  "source": {
    "id": 1649,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 267,
    "acip": "DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA DE YANG BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE BLTA BYA THUN MONG BA YIN PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA YIN GYI, YI DVAGS KYIS LAG TU BLANGS NAS LONGS SPYOD PAR BRTZAMS PA DE'I TSE NA KHYAD CHOS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA MA YIN TE, DE'I TSE NA YI DVAGS KYI THUN MONG MA YIN PA'I LONGS SPYOD BYA YIN PAS, RIGS 'DRA PHYI MA RNAG KHRAG TU SKYE BA YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "de ltar 'byung ba de yang brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de blta bya thun mong ba yin pa'i dbang du byas pa yin gyi, yi dvags kyis lag tu blangs nas longs spyod par brtsams pa de'i tshe na khyad chos gsum ldan du skye ba ma yin te, de'i tshe na yi dvags kyi thun mong ma yin pa'i longs spyod bya yin pas, rigs 'dra phyi ma rnag khrag tu skye ba yin pa'i phyir",
    "english": "What we just described as happening is only with reference to where a glass of something wet and flowing is an object shared by the three different beings, as they look at it together. When the craving spirit himself though picks up the glass in his hand and begins to partake of its contents, the glass of liquid is no longer something that exists with three different parts. Since at this point it is something that the craving spirit is experiencing exclusively, its continuation starts being pus and blood.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA DE YANG BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE BLTA BYA THUN MONG BA YIN PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA YIN GYI, YI DVAGS KYIS LAG TU BLANGS NAS LONGS SPYOD PAR BRTZAMS PA DE'I TSE NA KHYAD CHOS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA MA YIN TE, DE'I TSE NA YI DVAGS KYI THUN MONG MA YIN PA'I LONGS SPYOD BYA YIN PAS, RIGS 'DRA PHYI MA RNAG KHRAG TU SKYE BA YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar 'byung ba de yang brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de blta bya thun mong ba yin pa'i dbang du byas pa yin gyi, yi dvags kyis lag tu blangs nas longs spyod par brtsams pa de'i tshe na khyad chos gsum ldan du skye ba ma yin te, de'i tshe na yi dvags kyi thun mong ma yin pa'i longs spyod bya yin pas, rigs 'dra phyi ma rnag khrag tu skye ba yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"What we just described as happening is only with reference to where a glass of something wet and flowing is an object shared by the three different beings, as they look at it together. When the craving spirit himself though picks up the glass in his hand and begins to partake of its contents, the glass of liquid is no longer something that exists with three different parts. Since at this point it is something that the craving spirit is experiencing exclusively, its continuation starts being pus and blood.\"}"
